00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.500 align:start position:0%
watching a fruit fly may not be as  captivating as catching Tom Cruise's in  Top Gun but it turns out Cruz has  nothing on the insect when it comes to  doing complicated fighter jet maneuvers  like bank turns scientists didn't even  know that gypsophila high di flies could  turn quickly by rolling in midair until  a team put the insects in a miniature  stadium with a Jumbotron  the screen created the illusion of  something scary coming toward the flies  researchers use three infrared cameras  to see how the flies dodged to the  danger filming an infrared at 7,500  frames a second the team found that the  fly's bank and turned quickly much  faster than the way insects make small  routine turns where there's no danger  this sequence shows the Flies quick turn  at 1/3 hundredths of its natural speed  the fly is veering away from the scary  shape approaching from the bottom right  from the footage the team also created a  computer simulation of the same escape  turn slow to 1 300 the speed and  magnified twice the insect size the  rolling motions became much clearer the  fly rolls its whole body to one side  like a plane raising one of its wings as  it banks the fly then rolls back and  flies off the team reports the results  April 11th in science what's surprising  is that the fly doesn't have a lot of  fancy muscles or brainpower to maneuver  yet it doesn't seamlessly add a small  scale that miniaturization is something  scientists are still trying to  understand
 

